package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val setAetherdrift = StorySection(
    "dft", "Aetherdrift",
    "Ten teams from across the Multiverse raced through three worlds for a spark anyone could carry, and almost nobody played fair.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-the-grand-prix", "The Grand Prix",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-flag-drops", "The Flag Drops",
                    listOf(
                        "Aetherdrift came out on February 14, 2025, three days after it reached Magic Arena, under the set code DFT. Its two Commander decks shipped the same day under their own code, DRC. It was the hundred and third expansion in the game's history and the first Standard set of that year, and it took Magic somewhere it had never quite gone before, onto a racetrack. The set symbol is a waving chequered flag.",
                        "In the story it sits in the Omenpath Era, the stretch of years after the Phyrexian [invasion](invasion-of-the-multiverse) when the doors between worlds stayed open and most planeswalkers lost their sparks. It follows the horror of [Duskmourn](duskmourn) and comes before [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm), and it carries threads from both. A child stolen in the House of Duskmourn rides caged on a demon's car, and a dragon bursts out of a storm over the finish line. The short version of the same race lives in the root Lore under [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift). This page is the long one.",
                        "The idea is older than the Omenpaths. Mark Rosewater, who led its vision design, has said a death race set was talked about for more than a decade, and that travel between worlds was what finally let it happen. Its inspirations were Death Race 2000, Speed Racer, Wacky Races and The Fast and the Furious, films where winning means getting rid of the competition as much as going fast. Its codename in development was Tennis. Rosewater calls it a travelogue set, because its mechanics are about the race while the art and names on most cards are about the scenery the race runs through.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-sprint", "The Ghirapur Sprint",
                    listOf(
                        "The race began as a crime. For decades before the Omenpaths, the Ghirapur Sprint ran at night beside the Inventors' Fair, an unsanctioned dash through the city from checkpoint to checkpoint. Racers carried itineraries and had them marked at every stop, and the winner was the first across the last checkpoint with every mark in place. It drew couriers, aether smugglers, kinetic artists, daring engineers, pilots and every other kind of misfit who wanted to test the limits of their machines and themselves.",
                        "The people who ran it called themselves the New Culture Collective. They were radicals who held that whatever the Consulate forbade was worth doing, and they put out manifestos from behind masks and false names while agitating in student groups, engineers' councils and factory floors. The Sprint was their best recruiter. The Consulate spent fortunes trying to infiltrate it, and some years it arrested the organizers and called the whole affair a racket for gamblers and gangsters. That was not entirely wrong. The Sprint lost money every year, and the Collective took funding from Ghirapur's criminal underworld to keep it going.",
                        "When the Phyrexians came, the Collective set the race aside. Its safehouses, its maps of the city's back streets and its cells of fast, resourceful drivers became the plane's evacuation and supply network, and the Collective proved vital to the defense of the world. After the invasion it helped topple the Consulate in the Indigo Revolution and became a recognized political party with seats in the new Assembly. The Sprint was renamed the Ghirapur Grand Prix and handed to an independent governing body overseen by the city's night minister, Gonti, an aetherborn who had once run the black market.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Indigo Revolution changed more about Avishkar than just its name. Even Gonti's infamous night markets had gone legitimate.",
                        "Night Market",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-a-race-of-worlds", "A Race of Worlds",
                    listOf(
                        "The race in Aetherdrift is the second Grand Prix to cross between worlds and the second to be legal. The first had been a wild run across nearly two dozen planes. This one was a festival on a scale the Multiverse had never seen, a week long, with thousands of staff, engineers and marshals working across several planes, diplomatic teams negotiating the route, and a broadcast carried by omencast to watchers everywhere. Below the ten premier teams ran dozens of lesser divisions, from one day sprints for amateurs to GP Aspire, where twenty teams fought for a place in next year's field.",
                        "The course was fixed. It left from the heart of Ghirapur at nightfall, in honour of the old Sprint, and ran out to the city's edges and through a stable Omenpath to Amonkhet, along the green banks of the revived Luxa. Another path carried it into the wilds of Muraganda on an elevated track above the canopy, then back to Amonkhet for a sprint across the boiling desert, and home to Ghirapur for the final stage. Checkpoints logged every team that crossed them, so cheating was impossible on paper. The rule for winning was as plain as the old one. First across the line wins.",
                        "For Avishkar the race was never only a sport. The new government meant to make the plane a power in the Multiverse, and the Grand Prix was its showcase, proof that Avishkar could run logistics, diplomacy and spectacle across worlds better than anyone. It even spread the plane's language. The way race fans sorted Omenpaths into grades caught on far beyond Avishkar, and so did its standards for refining and measuring aether.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Ten teams. Ten dreams. One winner.",
                        "Starting Column",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-prize", "The Prize",
                    listOf(
                        "The Grand Prix's prize changed every year and was drawn from the hoard of treasures the old Consulate had kept hidden. This year it was {The Aetherspark}, an artificial planeswalker's spark held in an ornate vessel of chilled aether. It had been made during the invasion by an inventor named Ishikari Bindra, who built a net to catch a piece of the Blind Eternities while the Phyrexians had them torn open, and it was found in her workshop afterward.",
                        "To Avishkar's government it was a curiosity. Omenpaths were everywhere and proven machines already carried trade and diplomats through the stable ones, so nobody in the Assembly cared to copy it. The guide to the race calls it a relic of a bygone era, the perfect trophy because it bundled prestige, aether, risk and the whole Multiverse into a single object. For most of the people who wanted it, though, the reasoning ran the other way. In a world where nearly every planeswalker had lost the spark, a spark you could hold in your hand was priceless.",
                        "[Chandra Nalaar](who/chandra) wanted it for Nissa Revane, who had lost her spark and could no longer go home to Zendikar. A demon wanted it and sent a damned man to fetch it. Two former planeswalkers came to Ghirapur meaning to take it without racing at all. The card shows it as an Equipment that is also a planeswalker, gathering loyalty when the creature carrying it deals combat damage, which is to say it grows only for whoever is winning.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Aetherdrift, Part 1, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episode 1, The Dashing and the Desperate, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ghirapur Grand Prix"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-the-host-planes", "The Host Planes",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-avishkar", "Avishkar",
                    listOf(
                        "The plane once called Kaladesh had already had one revolution. In the Aether Revolt the Renegades threw down a tyrannical head consul and put a reformed Consulate in his place, and for a while people cheered. But the new Consulate changed very little. The courts and councils were buried in backlog, the old conservatives were still inside the government, and life for most people went on much as before. Discontent was rising on both the left and the right when the Phyrexians came under [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn).",
                        "The invasion did not defeat Avishkar, but it broke the Consulate's back. The government had ignored warnings of what was coming, and when it came, district mutual aid groups, criminal gangs and radicals filled the gap and did the fighting. Afterward the victors would not accept a return to the old order. In the Indigo Revolution, a largely bloodless handover driven by radical consuls, the New Culture Collective and a crowd of labour, peasant and student groups, the surviving consuls stepped down. The grievances that united everyone were the failure to prepare for the invasion and the unfair way aether had been shared out.",
                        "The new government, an elected Assembly drawn from the plane's eleven districts, renamed the world Avishkar, a word meaning invention, to shed the weight of the monarchy and both Consulates. Its mandate was double, to rebuild and to make Avishkar a power the Multiverse would have to respect. It opened outposts and embassies on other planes, sent money, goods and celebrities through the paths, and picked Ravnica, the only other world with a single plane-wide government worth the name, as its chief rival. Not an enemy, the guide notes. Yet.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "As Kaladesh became Avishkar, aether finally started flowing freely to all.",
                        "Aetherflux Conduit",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-three-kinds-of-path", "Three Kinds of Path",
                    listOf(
                        "Avishkar's great piece of luck was that Ghirapur ended up ringed with stable [Omenpaths](the-omenpaths), and its greatest export turned out to be a way of talking about them. The official Avishkari classification was never meant for scholars or engineers. It was a quick, popular system built from the slang of race commentators and fans, and it sorted every path into three grades.",
                        "Evergreen paths opened in the wake of the invasion and have never closed. The three that carry the Grand Prix, Avishkar to Amonkhet, Amonkhet to Muraganda and Avishkar to Muraganda, are all evergreen. Deciduous paths open and close on a regular, predictable cycle, like seasons, and many of Avishkar's neighbours are joined to it this way, Alacria and Kylem among them. Instanced paths are everything else, from new paths still being measured to ones that will never return. The path to Gastal stayed open for days. The one to [Duskmourn](duskmourn) opened for a few seconds. The Speedbrood, the Goblin Rocketeers, the Guidelight Voyagers and the sharkfolk of the Keelhaulers all reached Avishkar through instanced paths, and none of them could simply go home.",
                        "That is the quiet tragedy behind several teams in the race. Half the field are refugees of a sort, stranded by doors that closed behind them, and they race on Avishkar because Avishkar is where they landed. The grades caught on all across the Multiverse anyway, which the Assembly counted as its first victory in a new contest of influence between worlds.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-amonkhet-reborn", "Amonkhet Reborn",
                    listOf(
                        "Amonkhet had suffered the worst the Multiverse had to offer. [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) ruled it as the God-Pharaoh, twisted its gods and hid its history, then came the War of the Spark and then the Phyrexians. During the invasion the god Hazoret stood beside the Locust God and the Scarab God in Naktamun, and the living and the dead fought together. The Hekma, the barrier that had sealed the city off from the desert, collapsed. The defense held anyway, and when it was done the two insect gods withdrew into the Broken Lands.",
                        "Then the river Luxa rose, flooded the city and washed away the blood, oil and machine corpses. After the flood came rain, then frogs, then sun, and green life returned. The dead of Amonkhet still walk, because the ancient Curse of Wandering keeps them moving until they crumble to dust, but they do not grow and they do not have children. The undead of Naktamun understood that without the living the plane would one day simply end. So they made the living a promise. Free us, and we will keep Naktamun safe. The living agreed, and freed dead and living together held the walls against the wandering dead of the desert.",
                        "Now the dead raise orphans, tend fields and teach. A new holy book, the Record of Days, has replaced the God-Pharaoh's lies, and it names three gods. Hazoret, now called Godseeker, takes elite warriors into the Broken Lands to scout. Sab-Sunen, mother of the Luxa, a woman with a golden frog's head, appeared at the height of the celebrations after the flood. Ketramose, a young god of dawn, solidarity and the common people with a golden lion's head, rose from the reeds at the river's edge carrying a bow like Oketra's. Amonkhet entered the Grand Prix to show it was not a dead plane, and its people lined the course to cheer in hope instead of fear.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Let the past die so the future might live.",
                        "Ketramose, the New Dawn",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-chitin-court", "The Chitin Court",
                    listOf(
                        "Not all of Amonkhet's dead wanted a future with the living in it. Out in the Broken Lands lie tombs of kings, queens and emperors who reigned long before Bolas, and the invasion woke many of them. These are the monarchal dead, or simply monarchs, and they came up out of the sand to find their thrones crowded with descendants and rivals and a strange city thriving by the river. They fight each other across the desert for the right to rule the plane, and many of the unsatisfied ones gather at the Tombs Before Time, a far region of ruins where councils of dead rulers sit on dusty thrones and plot.",
                        "Nearly all of them swear loyalty to the Chitin Court. That is what the Locust God and the Scarab God now call themselves, the two survivors of a family of insect gods, and they mean to find the remains of their dead siblings and raise them. Their worshippers are the monarchs, the last lazotep Eternals and the countless dead of the Broken Lands, enough to keep them divine but not enough to make them strong again. To them the new gods are abominations, either made by Bolas outright, as Hazoret was, or born of a land he poisoned.",
                        "Racers and citizens whisper about the signs. Shed insect skins flowing through the dunes like a dry river. The silhouette of a god in the heart of a sandstorm. Ancient tombs found broken open from the inside, the sarcophagi empty and the weapon racks disturbed. None of it has yet reached the ears of Naktamun's leaders. The guide promises that it will.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Chitin Court lurks, awaiting its chance to reclaim Amonkhet from the living.",
                        "Cryptbreaker",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-muraganda", "Muraganda",
                    listOf(
                        "Muraganda is a primordial world, but it was not always one. Long ago, beyond living memory but recorded in glyphs that survivors carved and their descendants still keep, a civilization that spanned the plane discovered magic and then collapsed. The plane's moons shattered, and pieces of them still fall. Over the generations the moonfalls slowed, survivors formed bands and kingdoms, and raw magic, oozes and the craters left a world of jungle, volcanoes, wild leylines and beasts of every size. Ruins of the old civilization lie half buried under it all.",
                        "It was the only host plane without a team in the race, and it did not want the race. Avishkari advance teams arrived a year early and carved a road, sometimes literally, through jungle and coral pits and past sky-high geysers. They had a treaty with the Saurid Autocracy, the plane's mightiest state, but it turned out to be a treaty with a map. The terrain, the beasts, the oozes and the peoples of the land had signed nothing, and the Autocracy had cannily used the deal to push its own borders along the route. The guide says the plane answered the race the way a body answers an infection.",
                        "Avishkari researchers who studied the place found a strange thing. Its wildly varied creatures were not the product of evolution but of raw magic. Its oozes are stranger still. They range from droplets to walking lakes and take the shape of whatever they consume, and the ones that have swallowed thinking beings carry a faint echo of their minds. The greatest of them is the {Mimeoplasm, Revered One}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Under moonfall conditions, there's nothing you can do but drive and hope for the best.",
                        "Skycrash",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-peoples-of-muraganda", "Peoples of Muraganda",
                    listOf(
                        "The Saurid Autocracy is a kingdom of saurids, humanoid dinosaurs, ruling over human subjects they call softskins. It is a rigid pyramid, with lavish tyrant families at the top, squabbling bureaucrats, priests and generals in the middle, labouring saurids below, and softskins beneath them all, everyone subject to the autarch, revered as a divine monarch. The Autocracy built the plane's first paved roads and its canals and pit mines. Saurids worship oozes as the remains of their ancestors and feed their dying elders to them so they may live forever in the holy jelly. {Tyrox, Saurid Tyrant} shows how they treat a treaty.",
                        "In the jungles live the Fang Druids, mostly great apes with some humans among them, with no ranks by species, only respect for age and wisdom. They guard the Green Hell, a jungle grown among ancient war craters. On the Autocracy's border are the Telu-Set, five human clans who fled the magic-torn ruin of their homeland Telu. They are superb riders, smiths and weavers, and they hate magic, allowing only null magic that cancels other spells, and they hunt the mages of the ley towers.",
                        "The ley mages are sorcerers who build towers on leyline geysers and rarely share what they learn, served by glyphunters who search the moonfall fields for relics. Around Galadak's Scar, a cold field of the first moon craters, live scar witches, pale fading ghosts of the old world bound to patrons called veiled walkers or deadgods. The gold-clad Dawnchaser Pilgrims follow the old roads with their altars on wheels, worshipping the dawn, and the Wandering Scribes have invented paper. When the race came, a few mage circles reached out in curiosity. Most of Muraganda met it with swords drawn.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Fang Druids call it the World Tyrant, for it has consumed entire ecosystems that it keeps suspended in its core. To look upon it is to see the world as it was eons ago.",
                        "Terrian, World Tyrant",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Aetherdrift, Part 2, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Avishkar"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Muraganda"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-the-ten-teams", "The Ten Teams",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-home-teams", "The Home Teams",
                    listOf(
                        "The Aether Rangers race for Avishkar and its Assembly. They bring humans, elves, vedalken and dwarves together, old engineers who remember the Consulate beside young drivers full of the revolution's hope, and their machines are light, strong and built to fly over trouble on currents of aether. They are co-led by Chief Engineer Pia Nalaar, hero of two revolutions, and by Spitfire, a masked street racer whom the city adores and nobody can identify. The Assembly also funds a string of lower division Ranger squads. In the set they are green and blue, built around exhaust and ramp, with {Sita Varma, Masked Racer} as their captain card.",
                        "The Champions of Amonkhet race for a plane that wants to prove it is alive. Amonkhet's people know toil, and in the future they are building they have chosen to make room for sport and play and to raise up heroes who do not swing swords. Their captain is Zahur, an undead leonin charioteer from the lost era before the God-Pharaoh, rewrapped and dressed in fine cloth, with Basri Ket, who lost his spark, at his side. Their crew is living and dead together, in chariots pulled by undead river hippos.",
                        "In the set the Champions are white and black, playing for max speed and attrition, with {Zahur, Glory's Past} as captain. The common land {Scoured Barrens} carries their creed in its flavor text, that they race to show the Multiverse their home is a living world just beginning to wake. The Rangers' great rival is the reigning champion, and the Rangers had a particular reason to take that personally. Pia's own daughter, [Chandra](who/chandra), had signed with the other side.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Indigo Revolution ousted the Consulate once and for all. Now the citizens of Avishkar cheer for the Aether Rangers as they try to bring victory and glory to their new nation.",
                        "Thornwood Falls",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-cloudspire-and-voyagers", "Cloudspire and Voyagers",
                    listOf(
                        "The Cloudspire Racing Team came from Kylem, the plane of contests and arenas, and moved to Avishkar when the Omenpaths opened. They surprised everyone by winning the first interplanar Grand Prix. They are about pedigree, professionalism and personality, with stars for drivers and machines built to awe, branding as loud as their racing, and a habit of never being first to cross the line into open violence and never being first to back down. Rubbing is racing, they say. Their captain from last year, Kolodin, is a fine speaker and a team player, and he took on a co-captain this year with as little friction as he could manage.",
                        "That co-captain was [Chandra Nalaar](who/chandra), and her signing sent shockwaves through Avishkar, which had assumed she would race beside her mother. She did it because Cloudspire were the champions and she meant to win the Aetherspark for Nissa. In the set Cloudspire are red and white, aggressive vehicles and mounts, led by {Kolodin, Triumph Caster}.",
                        "The Guidelight Voyagers are robots, fully metal and run by programming rather than magic, which is why the set used the Robot creature type for the first time in a Magic world. They are limbs of a single mind that calls itself Mendicant Core. An Omenpath opened around them and dropped them on Avishkar after the invasion, and they want to go home. Their plan is to race the Omenpaths again and again, changing one thing at a time, hoping to recreate in reverse whatever brought them. Last year they finished mid pack. This year they began trying new and strange things, as if searching for something at the edge of the race. In the set they are white and blue artifacts, led by {Mendicant Core, Guidelight}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Guidelight Voyagers had been stranded on Avishkar with no way to get home. They considered every last unit essential, down to the glitchiest grunt.",
                        "Voyager Quickwelder",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-demons-and-rocketeers", "Demons and Rocketeers",
                    listOf(
                        "The Speed Demons are possessed racers in specter-haunted cars, kept in line by the demon that lets them leave their home. That home is the House of [Duskmourn](duskmourn), and the demon riding among them, {The Speed Demon}, is the tormentor of their captain. He is Winter, a survivor of the House who was granted leave by its archdemon Valgavoth to bring back the Aetherspark. Win, and he might be free. Lose, and he would be dragged back to face Valgavoth's anger. His ace was Loot, a small creature who could find any Omenpath, held in a cage. In the set they are blue and black, artifacts that drain the opponent, led by {Winter, Cursed Rider}.",
                        "The Goblin Rocketeers fell onto Avishkar from an unknown world through a path that was open for only a moment. Their co-captain Redshift insists it was no accident. Back home, goblins worshipped the BOOSTGOD and feared his enemy, a dead-stop deity whose name cannot be spoken, who holds the world under a velocity limit. Redshift's crew built the strongest rockets goblinkind had ever made and punched clean through that limit into another world.",
                        "On Avishkar they found people who worshipped speed without knowing the BOOSTGOD's name, plus plenty of fuel, so they gave up exploring and set out to win the Grand Prix instead. Their money came largely from their other co-captain, Daretti, a goblin inventor also stranded on Avishkar. In the set they are red and green, an exhaust midrange deck, led by {Redshift, Rocketeer Chief}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "In lieu of brakes, the Rocketeers installed a second accelerator pedal.",
                        "Pedal to the Metal",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-quickbeasts-and-brood", "Quickbeasts and Brood",
                    listOf(
                        "The Alacrian Quickbeasts are the only team that races on muscle and bone. Quickbeasts are huge, intelligent creatures from Alacria, a plane joined to Avishkar by a seasonal path, and they eat aether, which lets them run fast for days between feedings. Each one carries the soul of a city district of Alacria, and riders train for years and may mount one only if the beast agrees. Then they are bound together as two halves of their district's legend. The team that came to Avishkar is led by the rider Caradora and the Quickbeast Lagorin, who were not bound to each other at first but were forced into partnership by tragedy back home.",
                        "In the set the Quickbeasts are green and white, mounts and vehicles, with {Caradora, Heart of Alacria} and {Lagorin, Soul of Alacria} for leaders. They came to win, the lifeland {Blossoming Sands} says, and also to make friends against the unknown dangers of an open Multiverse.",
                        "The Speedbrood are insectoid racers from a plane whose name nobody can translate, racing for reasons nobody can translate either. They are friendly in the garages, fond of singing in many tones at once, brewing tea and trading fine cloth. They are nocturnal and restless unless racing. Their cars are alive. At the end of a great racer's life, they shed their shell and go through a long metamorphosis to become a vehicle themselves, the highest honour the brood can give. Their captain is Aatchik, given the title radian for those near their fated end, and she raced hoping to prove herself ready for her own change. In the set they are black and green graveyard cards, led by {Aatchik, Emerald Radian}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The only honor greater than piloting a Speedbrood vehicle is becoming one yourself.",
                        "Broodheart Engine",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-keelhaulers-and-endriders", "Keelhaulers, Endriders",
                    listOf(
                        "The Keelhaulers are a rowdy crew of sharkfolk, called chordatans, with their fishfolk and human comrades, riding stripped down machines that are often just a bare aethercycle engine with a seat strapped on. Chordatans arrived on Avishkar's coasts through a passing path and found themselves at the top of the food chain. They sense aether as a call to adventure and glory and burn it as fuel. They field crews in every division, and their premier team is led by their brashest captain, Howler. They also hired the Avishkari pirate Kari Zev and her monkey Ragavan as guides. In the set they are blue and red, discard aggro, with {Captain Howler, Sea Scourge} in charge.",
                        "The Endriders come from Gastal, a dying world stuck in permanent sunset, crossed by nomads and scavengers who roar between rare oases. Water, food and peace were scarce there, but at the end of their world gasoline was plentiful. When the paths opened, Endrider gangs left Gastal without looking back, and then ran out of fuel almost everywhere they went, because most worlds had none to sell.",
                        "Far Fortune's gang was one of those, and they were lucky enough to run dry on Avishkar, where every machine runs on aether. Now they are a popular team of hard edged competitors who race to keep the tank full. In the set they are black and red, max speed aggro, led by {Far Fortune, End Boss}. Gastal's vampires came too, and the set remembers what they drank.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "To the Endriders, racing is worship. Their prayers rise on the howls of straining engines. Explosions punctuate their chorus of shouts, forming a hymn of glory to the open road and all roads to come.",
                        "Bloodfell Caves",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Story", "Planeswalker's Guide to Aetherdrift, Part 1, by Miguel Lopez, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aetherdrift, Limited archetypes"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-off-the-line", "Off the Line",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-inventor", "The Inventor",
                    listOf(
                        "The story begins during the invasion, in a rattling tower on the far edge of Ghirapur. Ishikari Bindra climbs the stairs while the sky burns. She had seen worlds beyond her own ever since the inventor Rashmi built the Planar Bridge, and she read and studied everything she could about them. Rashmi's answer had been a letter saying Ishikari had a unique mind for seeing past current technology, but that her methods made her unsuitable for recommendation. After that came the dreams, a blackened sky split red, and creatures attacking the city.",
                        "Her machine stands at the top of the tower, built from whatever she could find in defiance of the Consulate and good sense, shaped like overlapping fish scales hollowed out to their edges. It was meant to be powered by a natural mage as well as aether, and she is not a mage, so using it may kill her. She believes someone will remember that she was right, and that whoever finds her spark will do something wonderful with it.",
                        "She pulls the golden lever engraved with one of her favourite sayings. For a moment her mind becomes one with the [Blind Eternities](the-blind-eternities), and the story says she truly attains the unattainable. What she left behind became {The Aetherspark}. After the invasion it was found, displayed, and finally made the prize of a race.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-spitfire", "Spitfire",
                    listOf(
                        "Years later, Ghirapur's queen of underground racing gets stuck climbing the wall of her father's estate. Spitfire is Sita Varma, and her father is Mohar Varma, a former consul who still wears his robes and keeps a private chef to pretend nothing has changed. He loved her in his way. He gave her the same education as any son and taught her engineering and how to defend herself, but every gift came with the expectation that she would settle down as he wished. When Gonti's name came over the radio at breakfast, he hurled the radio across the room.",
                        "Sita hangs her mask behind a portrait her father commissioned before the revolution, a soft eyed girl in a sari who looks nothing like her. In the Aether Rangers' hidden garage, Pia Nalaar has tuned her a car with a ten cylinder aether inverter and golden storm pistons. Sita asks whether it was set up the way she liked it, meaning Pia's daughter, and Pia does not quite answer. The car had been built for someone else.",
                        "On race day Sita slipped out with help from Lalan, a young seamstress, and Pia handed her a brass racing suit inlaid with glowing tubes and three canisters of aether. Each one slowed time for her for about two seconds. Pia apologized that the sizing might be off. She had tried to adjust it.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Some seek thrills in watching the show. Some seek thrills in being the show.",
                        "Canyon Vaulter",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-chandras-reasons", "Chandra's Reasons",
                    listOf(
                        "[Chandra Nalaar](who/chandra) was not good at being famous at home. She tried to get tea in disguise, buried in Nissa's sweaters with a scarf over her face, and was recognized at once. A reporter asked why she needed the prize, and she joked that her father had once held the city record for speeding tickets, many of them earned with toddler Chandra on his lap. Then he asked how it felt to race against her mother, and she had to hide behind her tea. The truth was simple. She wanted to give Nissa back the ability to travel wherever she wanted, and to see Nissa's eyes light up the way they used to.",
                        "Nissa had something she wanted to say and kept putting it off. On the morning of the race Pia arrived with homemade samosas and a jab about little Chandra statues on every mantel, none of them in a Ranger uniform. Nissa offered that two of them racing meant twice the chance to win, and Pia decided she liked her.",
                        "Backstage, Kolodin gave a rousing speech, nearly as good as Ajani's, Chandra thought, and she could not listen. She could hear crying. She followed it past the goblins and the Voyagers to the Speed Demons' garage, where ghouls clanged at twisted cars and spirits pressed against glass. Winter was clamping a cage tighter around a fuzzy creature with a glowing tail, so small it had to stand on its hind legs. Chandra heated two bars until they glowed and bent them into a perch.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-collapse", "The Collapse",
                    listOf(
                        "A young man named Suraj Chaudry III had built the first viewing stand himself, his pride and joy, and saved money by using a cheaper copper alloy instead of steel. Gonti had approved it without a word. The night minister took the stage, fired the signal flare, and the racers shot out of the gates, the Rocketeers in front and Chandra oddly in last, tailing the Speed Demons. Then the crowd rose to its feet at once and the stand groaned and gave way.",
                        "Spitfire drove into chaos. Sharkfolk fired cannons, wasps swarmed her car, and the Voyagers jammed her radio. An Endrider threw a hooked chain at her and she braked so it caught a lamp post instead, and a zombie river horse trampled the chain and crushed the young rider's leg. Ahead lay falling stone, girders and rescue teams snatching spectators out of the air.",
                        "She slotted in her first canister and time all but stopped. She drifted around a rain of food from the concession stand, launched off the back of a Voyager's tank, fired auxiliary thrusters that were her idea and not Pia's, and dodged the Speed Demon's spectral claw. The floor of the platform was falling like a great disk, and the only way through was the hole left by its stairwell. She threaded it, landed three seconds later, and coasted through the [Omenpath](the-omenpaths) into the heat of Amonkhet. Every channel on Avishkar played it on a loop.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "An early lead puts a target on your back.",
                        "Broken Wings",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-visitors", "The Visitors",
                    listOf(
                        "Mohar Varma watched the replays with disgust. Chandra Nalaar, whatever her faults, had saved the Multiverse, and he had attended her medal ceremony himself. This masked upstart was, he assumed, some street kid Gonti had found. Then he heard someone in the house, took an aether blaster from the wall, and thanked the gods his daughter was not home to see violence.",
                        "In the hall stood a woman with green skin and tendrils for hair, and he found he could not pull the trigger or speak. A man in a blue cloak stepped out of the shadows with glowing eyes, and Mohar half remembered him from the Aether Revolt. It was [Jace Beleren](who/jace), with Vraska. Jace filled Mohar's head with a vision of the consuls restored to their halls, order returned, and a Sita who knew her place.",
                        "Then Jace offered to make it real, a bloodless revolution of their own, and Mohar began gathering allies. Jace wanted Loot back, the child taken from him and Vraska in Duskmourn, and the Aetherspark that might let Vraska travel again. He was ready to put an old government back on its throne to get them. The [Aetherdrift](aetherdrift) Lore article follows the plot from here, and so does the next tab.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episode 1, The Dashing and the Desperate, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episode 2, Pit Stop, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Sita Varma"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-the-finish-line", "The Finish Line",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-sand-and-jungle", "Sand and Jungle",
                    listOf(
                        "Amonkhet was brutal. Endriders threw flaming chainsaws, and one of Zahur's charioteers, a young man named Khuru, died taking a blow meant for his captain and was raised again on the spot. Winter burst into blue flame and his demon's claw knocked the Champions off the track. [Chandra](who/chandra) hit the Speed Demons with a fireball, heard Loot yelp in his cage, and was ashamed of herself. Spitfire took second place on the stage behind the Speed Demons, which made no sense to anyone.",
                        "Then a path carried the field into Muraganda, a world of trees as tall as buildings, rivers and boulders hanging in the air, and quakes whenever its moons fell. Sita, sheltering from a moonfall, remembered the invasion, her family's guards turned Phyrexian and a limb of the Invasion Tree crushing a neighbour's house. Lizardfolk raiders on dinosaurs ambushed the racers, and one of them hooked Loot's cage right off Winter's car.",
                        "Chandra left the race to go after him. Sita ran a canister up the spine of a dinosaur, and when Pia ordered her to turn back and help, Sita said her family had only ever slowed her down, and switched off her radio. In the raiders' camp Loot was sharing a cup of Fioran hot chocolate with Daretti, also a prisoner, who expected them both to be sacrificed. Chandra crept in, Pia arrived and flattened a raider, and they fled in Daretti's rocket car. Loot steered them at a tree whose bark fell away to show a hidden Omenpath.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Loot decided he liked these rescuers much more than the last ones.",
                        "Explosive Getaway",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-oasis", "The Oasis",
                    listOf(
                        "Loot's shortcut came out far from the track, and the engine did not survive the landing. They crashed at an oasis on Amonkhet. Daretti, who lived with chronic pain, shared his medicine. They rebuilt the car from palm fibre and hippo bones, with Chandra as the welding torch. By the fire she admitted why she had left her mother's team and broke down over Nissa, and Pia told her that nothing they made was ever wasted.",
                        "In the night a god sat down by the water. Chandra had believed [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) killed every god of Amonkhet but one. {Ketramose, the New Dawn} explained that wherever there is breath and water and people who face the dawn, Amonkhet will have gods. He cooled his feet in the oasis, told them the way back to the track and asked only to hear about other worlds. Chandra started with Zendikar.",
                        "Then Loot told them the truth. He knew where every Omenpath went, and Winter had forced him to show the secret ones, which was how the Speed Demons kept arriving ahead of everyone. Pia hotwired the rebuilt car, and with {Loot, the Pathfinder} giving directions they took one last shortcut home. Back on the track, Sita and Winter were side by side on the final straight into Ghirapur.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-trap", "The Trap",
                    listOf(
                        "Mohar's plan was a trap at the finish line, his guards seizing the course in front of the whole Multiverse. He had brought in a former consul named Harshad and Rudra, a disgraced captain of the old guard, who warned it might come to blood. Mohar said no revolution worth having had ever been bloodless. Vraska worried aloud about the ordinary people who would be hurt, and [Jace](who/jace) told her none of it would matter in a couple of weeks. He never said what he meant.",
                        "The track's golden safety barrier rose in front of the leaders, Sita's mask broke, and her father's men dragged her before him. Jace searched Winter's mind and took him prisoner as a loose end. Chandra's patched car fell out of the sky over Ghirapur into a finish held by Consulate guards. A dragon came out of a storm and attacked the city, and the city's defenses rose to meet it. Jace seized the minds of wrecked racers and set them on Chandra, who would not burn them.",
                        "A guard put a knife to Pia's throat. Sita spent her last canister, the one she had saved to win, breaking one guard's arm and dropping the other, and Daretti's drones buzzed overhead while Vin, the floating eyeball who called the race, broadcast every second.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Jace knew that if he just had time to explain his plan, he would find support. Unfortunately, time was in short supply.",
                        "Unstoppable Plan",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-after-the-flag", "After the Flag",
                    listOf(
                        "Jace struck Chandra with a spell that left her bleeding from the eyes, and even he looked shaken by it. He took Loot. Elspeth, now an archangel, fought the dragon, and Nissa rode a wave of vines to Chandra's side and begged Vraska not to go. Vraska went. She and Jace vanished into the fog. Winter crawled toward the line on a broken leg, and a door opened in the track and dragged him back into the House, screaming, as {The Last Ride} warned it would.",
                        "The race was won by the Champions of Amonkhet. Their chariot had been rebuilt by hundreds of hands, and Basri's sand shielded the crowd and turned to glass under the dragon's fire. {Zahur, Glory's Past} and his team lifted the Aetherspark together. Mohar Varma went to prison for trying to overthrow the government, and the coup he promised would be bloodless never became a revolution at all.",
                        "Elspeth healed Chandra and warned them the dragonstorms were spilling across the Omenpaths. Far away, on a cliff above Tarkir's steppes, Jace carried a sleeping Loot and walked ahead of Vraska into the place where the storms were born, and the story continues in [Tarkir Dragonstorm](tarkir-dragonstorm).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Their race ended much the same way it began, walking through a sandstorm on a world that ended long ago.",
                        "Ride's End",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Episodes 3 to 6, by K. Arsenault Rivera, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-side-stories", "Side Stories",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-into-the-broken-lands", "Into the Broken Lands",
                    listOf(
                        "Two side stories ran beside the race. The first, Hour of Restoration by Hadeer Elsbai, is set on Amonkhet in the weeks before the Grand Prix. Basri Ket turned up at the door of Mahitab, a woman from his old crop, with an offer. He wanted the body of Temmet, the champion once chosen by the god Oketra, and Mahitab and her companion Niharet were the last living members of the crew that had entombed him. Only they knew where.",
                        "Mahitab refused, until Niharet said it would be nice to have a home with windows. Niharet had once been branded a dissenter for speaking of forgotten gods, and the angels of the old order had wrapped her alive and sealed her in a sarcophagus. Mahitab freed her during the Hour of Devastation, but she never stopped waking up screaming in their windowless shack. So the two of them set out with Basri and a pair of fifteen year old twins, Marunaten and Merinaten, walking by night across the Broken Lands, steering by the stars.",
                        "The desert was too quiet. A sandwurm burst out of the dunes and swallowed Merinaten. Basri knocked it down with waves of sand, Mahitab drove her khopesh into its head, and they pulled the girl out of its teeth alive but badly hurt. Basri, who could not afford to pay for a second trip, carried her on his back the rest of the way.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-ketramose-rises", "Ketramose Rises",
                    listOf(
                        "At dawn they found a half buried pyramid. Basri lifted the sand from a hidden entrance Mahitab had marked long ago, and inside, in a single empty chamber, lay a sarcophagus. Mahitab saw Temmet's charred lazotep under his wrappings. Then a voice behind them said that was quite enough. Seven figures stood there, all but one of them dead, led by a skeletal warrior named Munhatep with a spear tipped by a half sun, loyal followers of the Chitin Court. And Niharet, the woman Mahitab loved, had her blade at Basri's throat.",
                        "Munhatep lifted Temmet like a grain of sand, and then the roof of the tomb tore open to the suns. A giant god with a golden lion's face stood over them holding Oketra's bow. The Chitin Court's arrows bounced off him. He plucked Temmet out of Munhatep's grip, threw the dead man aside, and in a blinding light made Temmet whole and free of corruption.",
                        "The god named himself Ketramose, son of Oketra, and told Temmet his work was unfinished and that together they would herald a new dawn. Then he walked toward Naktamun and faded into the desert. Niharet would not come home. The Chitin Court had welcomed her, she said, and she asked Mahitab to come with her. Mahitab refused, and watched her go. The restored champion became {Temmet, Naktamun's Will}, commander of the Eternal Might deck.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Each time he glimpsed oblivion, he learned another lesson.",
                        "Temmet, Naktamun's Will",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-like-no-other-beast", "Like No Other Beast",
                    listOf(
                        "The second side story, Like No Other Beast by Zachary Olson, belongs to Muraganda and is told as a pulp sword and sorcery tale. Its hero is Prince Khrad of the Telu-Set, son of King Khal, a mage hunter who rides with Grazak, a raptor who grows huge and glowing when fed the right magic pearl. A root oracle in Mon'Telu, the Telu-Set's stronghold, cried out about a strange new beast, and Khrad set out after it carrying the Star-Sword, a blade of shining white.",
                        "In a burning grove he found heaps of twisted metal and a band of masked raiders who breathed fire and swung burning clubs, and he and Grazak cut through them. Then he found the beast's trail, two deep grooves with no footprints. The beast turned out to be a race car, dragged through the jungle by a giant tar black ooze under the whip of the sorcerer Amarth-Tel. A young woman sat shackled in it, and Khrad was smitten at once.",
                        "A great ape druid named Chatal stopped Khrad in the jungle, told him the world was larger than he knew, and gave him a strange metal token on a ring with a little furry charm beside it. Khrad did not understand it, and marched on.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-tarry-spire", "The Tarry Spire",
                    listOf(
                        "Amarth-Tel's lair was the Tarry Spire, a tower at the edge of Kalaka's Scar where moonfall craters held bowls of black oil and the leylines bunched. Khrad stormed in, and a vast ooze rose from the floor, full of swirling skulls with an imperiosaur's at its heart. He and Grazak fought it until Grazak flung him into the air and he drove the Star-Sword through the central skull. At the top of the tower he found the captive, a pearl diver named Nathala, told her to hide, and ran off to kill the sorcerer.",
                        "Nathala was no helpless prisoner. Once Khrad was gone she dropped the smile, told Grazak plainly what she thought of his master, and explained that she meant to take the car, learn its secrets and share them with her people. The raptor could understand her far better than he ever understood Khrad. She climbed to the roof, put the key in the ignition and turned it.",
                        "The engine's roar lit a fire in Khrad's belly. Nathala ran Amarth-Tel down under the wheels, slid around the stunned prince, let Grazak into the passenger seat and, at the raptor's pleading, let Khrad into the back. They rode the car straight down the falling tower. When Khrad tried to claim the machine, Nathala looked at him with the same scorn he had shown the sorcerer, and he finally heard himself. She fired up the engine, he jumped into the back, and the three of them drove off together.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Avishkari delegation considered their treaties with the Saurid Autocracy settled, but that was a crucial misunderstanding. To the Autocracy, diplomacy is a matter of power, not paper; testing the Grand Prix's defenses is merely their way of making a counteroffer.",
                        "Tyrox, Saurid Tyrant",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Hour of Restoration, by Hadeer Elsbai, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Story", "Aetherdrift, Like No Other Beast, by Zachary Olson, Magic Story"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Ketramose"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Magic Story archive"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-mechanics", "Mechanics",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-speed", "Speed",
                    listOf(
                        "Speed is the set's big new idea, a resource that belongs to the player rather than to any card. A card with start your engines gives you speed if you have none, and it starts at 1. The first time an opponent loses life during each of your turns, your speed goes up by one. It never goes down and it never goes above 4. Once you have speed you keep it for the rest of the game, even if the card that gave it to you is gone, and the set came with a double sided helper card to track it.",
                        "At 4 you have max speed, and a long list of cards turn on. {Far Fortune, End Boss} pings every opponent whenever you attack, and at max speed adds one to every bit of damage your sources deal to opponents and their permanents. {Mendicant Core, Guidelight} copies your artifact spells for one extra mana. {Vnwxt, Verbose Host} doubles your draws. Even the lands get in on it. {Muraganda Raceway} taps for one colourless mana, or two once you are at max speed.",
                        "The design pushes you to hit the opponent early and every turn, which is exactly what a death race wants. The cost is that speed only climbs on your own turn and only once, so it takes at least three of your turns of drawing blood to get from 1 to 4, and the race to get there is half the game.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-exhaust", "Exhaust",
                    listOf(
                        "Exhaust is the set's new keyword for activated abilities. An exhaust ability can be activated only once for that permanent, ever, so it is a burst of power held in reserve, the nitro button on the dashboard. Exhaust appears on creatures, vehicles and artifacts in every colour, and it pairs naturally with anything that untaps, copies or reuses permanents.",
                        "{Loot, the Pathfinder} carries three of them at once. One adds three mana of a single colour, one draws three cards and one deals 3 damage to any target, and each can be used exactly once. {Redshift, Rocketeer Chief} has a single enormous one that drops any number of permanents from your hand onto the battlefield. {Sita Varma, Masked Racer} pours counters into herself and can then make every other creature you control as big as she is for a turn.",
                        "In Limited exhaust leans toward two of the teams. The Goblin Rocketeers play it as midrange in red and green, and the Aether Rangers use it in green and blue to ramp into their biggest threats.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-vehicles-and-mounts", "Vehicles and Mounts",
                    listOf(
                        "Of course there are vehicles, everywhere, in every colour and at every rarity. A vehicle is an artifact that becomes a creature when you crew it by tapping creatures with enough total power. Mounts came back from Outlaws of Thunder Junction, creatures you saddle by tapping other creatures, which lets them do more when they attack. Many cards care about having either one, and the set's Pilot tokens crew and saddle as though they had 2 more power.",
                        "The set worked hard to keep vehicles useful when you have no crew. A cycle of uncommon vehicles, {Detention Chariot}, {Spire Mechcycle} and {Earthrumbler} among them, all do something on their own. The legendary {Kolodin, Triumph Caster} gives your mounts and vehicles haste, saddles mounts as they enter and turns vehicles into creatures for the turn they arrive. {Lumbering Worldwagon} fetches lands every time it enters or attacks, and became a bundle promo.",
                        "Two of the ten Limited teams are built around them, the Alacrian Quickbeasts in green and white as midrange and Cloudspire in red and white as aggro, and each of the ten teams has an uncommon gold vehicle or mount as its signpost, {Thundering Broodwagon} and {Cloudspire Skycycle} among them.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "For Team Cloudspire, the track is merely a suggestion.",
                        "Cloudspire Skycycle",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-cycling-and-cameos", "Cycling and Cameos",
                    listOf(
                        "Cycling returned, and for once the name matched the theme. Pay its cost and discard the card to draw a new one. The set uses two kinds. Some cards simply cycle to smooth your draws. Others trigger when you cycle them, so they play almost like two cards in one. {Basri, Tomorrow's Champion} makes lifelink Cats, or cycles to give your Cats hexproof and indestructible for the turn. Discarding feeds the Keelhaulers' discard aggro deck and the Speedbrood's graveyard deck.",
                        "Each host plane got a few cameo mechanics. Amonkhet brought back embalm and exert, so {Cursecloth Wrappings} can give embalm to a creature card in your graveyard. Muraganda brought a nod to {Muraganda Petroglyphs}, the old card that rewards creatures with no abilities, in a cycle of uncommon legends with no rules text at all, the Tyrants, among them {Terrian, World Tyrant} and {Sundial, Dawn Tyrant}. Nothing from Kaladesh block's own mechanics came back to the main set.",
                        "The set also introduced the Seal creature type, used Robot for the first time in a Magic world for the Guidelight Voyagers, and arrived with one of the largest Oracle updates ever made. Around twelve thousand cards were changed to refer to themselves as this creature or this artifact instead of by name, and hundreds of legends got short names.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-ten-teams-in-limited", "Ten Teams in Limited",
                    listOf(
                        "Every two colour pair in the set is a racing team. White and blue are the Guidelight Voyagers, playing artifacts for value. Blue and black are the Speed Demons, artifacts that bleed the opponent. Black and red are the Endriders, racing to max speed. Red and green are the Goblin Rocketeers with exhaust midrange. Green and white are the Alacrian Quickbeasts with vehicles and mounts. Mark Rosewater has said the teams were divided less by creature type than by mood and tone.",
                        "The other five pairs complete the grid. White and black are the Champions of Amonkhet, max speed attrition. Blue and red are the Keelhaulers, discard aggro. Black and green are the Speedbrood, the graveyard deck. Red and white are Cloudspire, aggressive vehicles and mounts. Green and blue are the Aether Rangers, exhaust and ramp.",
                        "Each team has a rare gold captain, an uncommon gold vehicle or mount and a second uncommon gold signpost, and each colour pair has a common lifeland whose flavor text describes its team. The play booster box was cut from thirty six packs to thirty, and the old advertising card slot was dropped. Rosewater later admitted that the set did not do as well as Wizards had hoped.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Aetherdrift Mechanics, by Matt Tabak, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "You Get the Aetherdrift, Parts 1 and 2, by Mark Rosewater"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Speed"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-cards-worth-knowing", "Cards Worth Knowing",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-the-captains", "The Captains",
                    listOf(
                        "The ten rare gold captains are the heart of the set and a deep well of Commander legends. {Winter, Cursed Rider} gives your artifacts ward that costs 2 life to get past. {Zahur, Glory's Past} sacrifices creatures to surveil and, at max speed, leaves a Zombie behind whenever a nontoken creature of yours dies. {Caradora, Heart of Alacria} finds you a mount or vehicle and adds an extra +1/+1 counter whenever counters land on your creatures or vehicles.",
                        "{Captain Howler, Sea Scourge} turns every discard into a pump and a card draw. {Aatchik, Emerald Radian} fills the board with Insects from your graveyard and drains each opponent whenever one dies. {Redshift, Rocketeer Chief} makes mana for activated abilities only and holds an exhaust that empties your hand onto the battlefield. {Sita Varma, Masked Racer} turns every creature you control into a copy of her own size. {Kolodin, Triumph Caster}, {Far Fortune, End Boss} and {Mendicant Core, Guidelight} round out the ten.",
                        "Alongside them came five mono coloured rare legends, among them {Gonti, Night Minister}, who steals cards whenever creatures hit your opponents, {Daretti, Rocketeer Engineer}, who recycles artifacts from your graveyard, and {Oviya, Automech Artisan}, who puts creatures and vehicles straight into play.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-gods-and-mythics", "Gods and Mythics",
                    listOf(
                        "Amonkhet's three gods are mythic legends. {Ketramose, the New Dawn} is indestructible with menace and lifelink, draws a card whenever things are exiled from graveyards or the battlefield on your turn, and can only fight once seven cards are in exile. {Hazoret, Godseeker} is a two mana indestructible haste god who cannot attack until you reach max speed. {Sab-Sunen, Luxa Embodied} grows every turn, drawing two cards on the odd turns and fighting only on the even ones.",
                        "{Loot, the Pathfinder} was the set's mascot and one of its strongest Commander cards, a three colour body with double strike, vigilance, haste and three exhaust abilities. {Chandra, Spark Hunter} is the set's planeswalker, animating a vehicle every combat and making vehicle tokens of her own. {Mimeoplasm, Revered One} eats creature cards from your graveyard and can become any of them. {Mu Yanling, Wind Rider} gives all your vehicles flying, and {The Last Ride} is a one mana vehicle that grows as your life shrinks.",
                        "There was also a cycle of two colour Gearhulks in the allied pairs, continuing the cycle from the original Kaladesh, such as {Brightglass Gearhulk}, which tutors two cheap cards. [Chandra](who/chandra), [Jace](who/jace) and the rest of the story turn up across the rares and the story spotlight cards, from {Explosive Getaway} to {Unstoppable Plan}.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The rearview mirror showed Winter what awaited him should he fail.",
                        "The Last Ride",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-lands-and-tyrants", "Lands and Tyrants",
                    listOf(
                        "The set's rare lands were a new cycle of verges in the enemy pairs, such as {Bleachbone Verge}, each tapping for one colour and for a second if you control a matching basic land type. Their flavor texts all have characters talking about Loot. The uncommon roads, such as {Country Roads}, enter tapped unless you control a mount or vehicle, and can be sacrificed for a Pilot token.",
                        "A small vertical cycle charts the course. {Avishkar Raceway} at common is stages one and five in the streets of Ghirapur, {Amonkhet Raceway} at uncommon is stages two and four in the sands and waters of Amonkhet, and {Muraganda Raceway} at rare is stage three in the steaming jungle. Their rarity rises in the order the race reaches them.",
                        "The five Tyrants are uncommon legendary creatures from Muraganda with no abilities at all, huge bodies made for vanilla synergies. {Terrian, World Tyrant} is green, {Tyrox, Saurid Tyrant} is red, {Kalakscion, Hunger Tyrant} is black, {Caelorna, Coral Tyrant} is blue and {Sundial, Dawn Tyrant} is white. Their flavor texts carry some of the best Muraganda lore in the set, from a coral giant that grew for centuries under the sea to a crater predator that eclipses every other monster in Galadak's Scar.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "Stage 3: The steaming jungles of Muraganda under falling skies.",
                        "Muraganda Raceway",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-special-treatments", "Special Treatments",
                    listOf(
                        "The set had a lot of dressing up. Borderless Revved Up cards, Rude Riders and Graffiti Giants each gave a different look, and the legendary drivers got their own borderless frames. Full art Driver's Seat basic lands put you behind the wheel. A new first place foil appeared only in box topper packs, one in every play booster box, collector box and Finish Line bundle, alongside full art Panorama lands.",
                        "{The Aetherspark} was the set's Headliner, with a serialized version. Ten Japan Showcase cards, reimagined by Japanese artists in a frame inspired by Japanese hobby shops, came in collector boosters, some in fracture foil. The buy a box promo was {Lifecraft Engine}, and the bundle promo was {Lumbering Worldwagon}.",
                        "The ten Special Guests were picked by the Secret Lair team, among them {Chrome Mox}, {Galvanic Blast}, {Whir of Invention}, {Thoughtcast}, {Chandra's Ignition} and {Skysovereign, Consul Flagship}, chosen to fit the race's mood of multiplanar chaos. The Standard Showdown promo was a {Lightning Strike} with Cowboy Bebop art. The Special Guests could turn up in ordinary play boosters too, about one pack in sixty four, and they were legal to draft and play in Limited like any other card in the box.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Collecting Aetherdrift, by Jubilee Finnegan and Max McCall"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aetherdrift"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "dft-commander-decks", "Commander Decks",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-living-energy", "Living Energy",
                    listOf(
                        "Aetherdrift had only two Commander decks, both released February 14, 2025, under the code DRC and designed by a team led by Ethan Fleischer. Neither one is about vehicles or speed. Instead they lean into the host worlds, into things the main set had no room for. Each deck holds ten new cards plus a foil borderless commander and a second foil borderless legend.",
                        "Living Energy is the Avishkar deck, green, blue and red, about energy, artificers and artifact creatures. Its face is {Saheeli, Radiant Creator}, who gives you energy whenever you cast an artificer or artifact spell and spends three energy each combat to copy a permanent as a hasty 5/5 artifact creature. Beside her stands [Chandra's](who/chandra) mother, {Pia Nalaar, Chief Mechanic}, who gets energy when your artifact creatures connect and turns it into flying Aetherjet vehicles.",
                        "The new cards include {Nissa, Worldsoul Speaker}, {Aetherflux Conduit}, {Aetheric Amplifier}, {Peema Trailblazer}, {Rampaging Aetherhood} and {Stridehangar Automaton}. Old Kaladesh favourites fill out the list, {Aetherworks Marvel}, {Panharmonicon} and {Whirler Virtuoso} among them. It plays like a tour of the inventors' fair, a deck that builds a pile of machines, banks energy from every one of them, and then spends it all at once on something far bigger than any single card in the box.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The Omenpaths didn't just open portals to other worlds. It opened the eyes of many inventors who had been thinking too small.",
                        "Chromatic Lantern",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-eternal-might", "Eternal Might",
                    listOf(
                        "Eternal Might is the Amonkhet deck, white, blue and black, about Zombies, discard and the eternalize mechanic. Its face is {Temmet, Naktamun's Will}, the champion Ketramose raised in the side story, a Zombie wizard with vigilance and menace who loots whenever you attack and pumps your Zombies whenever you draw. Its second legend is {Hashaton, Scarab's Fist}, who turns discarded creature cards into 4/4 Zombie copies.",
                        "The new cards include {Lost Monarch of Ifnir}, {Prophet of the Scarab}, {Priest of the Crossing}, {Rhet-Tomb Mystic}, {On Wings of Gold}, {Renewed Solidarity}, {Wizened Mentor} and {Accursed Duneyard}. The reprints are a tour of Amonkhet's dark history, with {The Scarab God}, {God-Pharaoh's Gift} and {Liliana, Death's Majesty}, the card of the necromancer [Liliana](who/liliana) who once bargained with [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas) and in the end turned his own undead army against him.",
                        "Its double sided tokens pair plain Zombies with eternalized copies of creatures in the deck, so a reborn {Champion of Wits} sits on the back of an ordinary Zombie. It is a deck that wants cards moving through the hand and into the graveyard all game, then marching back out of it, which is a fair description of Amonkhet's dead in the Omenpath Era as well.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote(
                        "The edge of Naktamun was once protected by Kefnet. The honored mummies who patrol it now do so in his image.",
                        "On Wings of Gold",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "dft-why-only-two", "Why Only Two",
                    listOf(
                        "Most sets of that era came with four Commander decks, and Aetherdrift had two. Gavin Verhey explained that the choice answered players who felt there were simply too many Commander decks coming out. Fewer decks meant each could be more focused, and both went to the planes the main set treated as scenery.",
                        "That gives the two decks an odd relation to the race. Living Energy is full of the Avishkari inventors who built the cars, and Eternal Might is full of the dead the Champions of Amonkhet raced beside, yet neither plays a single vehicle as its theme. Mark Rosewater said the decks were deliberately about things specific to those worlds rather than the set's mechanics.",
                        "For anyone building around the race itself, the captains, the gods and {Loot, the Pathfinder} from the main set carry that load instead. The decks also kept energy, the counter Kaladesh first introduced, alive as a Commander theme when the main set left it out, and they kept embalm's bigger cousin eternalize alive too, so both of the old worlds got to bring their own machinery to the table even while the race itself ran on speed and exhaust.",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Aetherdrift Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Aetherdrift Commander Decklists, by Jubilee Finnegan, on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki, Aetherdrift Commander"),
                StorySource("Reference", "Scryfall"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
